# Station Lanes with a pod set (strap-ons): the pod is added from the parts bin and shows in Structure as `Pod ×3` (a link
# glyph, like a symmetry group) with its own nose and tube under it; parts placed on the pod appear in their own lanes
# bracketed `Pod ×N`. Run with the example rocket:
#   cargo run -q -p bowshock -- drive --example crates/bowshock/scripts/lanes_pods.drive /tmp/lanes-pods
viewport 1280x800
scale 3
click (160, 88)
type "pod"
click "Pod / strap-on"
move (900, 300)
screenshot pod-added.png
# Hover the pod's body tube: it is named with its station and its pod.
move (873, 628)
screenshot pod-hover.png
# Select the pod's body tube from its bar (the 3D view and the inspector follow).
click (873, 628)
screenshot pod-member-selected.png
# A strap-on needs a tube wide enough for a motor: widen the pod's tube (the pod set carries every copy), then the bin's Inner tube
# lands in it (the search spans every category, so it is typed over).
click (1175, 167)
type "60"
type enter
click (160, 88)
type backspace
type backspace
type backspace
type "inner tube"
click "Inner tube"
screenshot pod-inner-tube.png
# Make it a motor mount: its bar moves to Propulsion, bracketed `Pod ×3` with the other parts of the pod there, the tag on the
# row that is free.
click "Holds a motor"
move (640, 560)
screenshot pod-mount.png
# (The motor bar of a strap-on's mount, `H128W ×3`, is covered by the unit tests in lanes/scene.rs and lanes/pods.rs: the bin
# lists only motors that fit the pod tube's bore and length, which the drive script cannot size from here.)
